 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Multi-Space Transfer Function Blending (Heuristic Modulation)
-A heuristic approach for combining multiple transfer functions defined in multiple spaces by mapping them to a single common space and using an extra dimension to modulate color intensity. This method is used when true multi-dimensional blending (up to 3D) is not feasible or when reducing complexity is desired. It maps all histograms to a common space (e.g., 1D or 2D) and uses an additional dimension (or intensity modulation) to handle the combination. In the 2D case, the extra dimension is used to modulate the intensity of the color. This is done by determining the number of scalar values within a 2D widget and the number outside, and using this fraction to reduce the intensity accordingly. Though not as powerful as true multi-dimensional transfer functions, this heuristic produces acceptable results and allows all histograms to be reduced to a common space [S1, unofficial].
+A heuristic approach for combining multiple transfer functions defined in multiple spaces by mapping them to a single common space and using an extra dimension to modulate color intensity [S1, unofficial]. It is used when true multi-dimensional blending is not feasible or when a simpler, common-space representation is preferred [S1, unofficial]. To implement: determine the number of scalar values within a 2D widget and the number outside [S1, unofficial]. Use the fraction of values inside vs. outside to reduce the intensity accordingly [S1, unofficial]. This is not as powerful as true multi-dimensional transfer functions but produces acceptable results and allows all histograms to be reduced to a common space [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** O(1) per sample during rendering, as the blending is pre-computed into a 3D texture. The cost is in the initial construction of the 3D LUT, which depends on the resolution of the histogram widgets.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** O(N^3) for the 3D lookup table, where N is the resolution of each dimension. This is constrained by the 3D hardware limit.
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation using standard 3D texture sampling; intuitive blending of up to three distinct features; compatible with existing GPU hardware.
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot handle more than three unique data dimensions simultaneously; less expressive than true higher-dimensional transfer functions; heuristic modulation sacrifices precision for compatibility.
-*   **When to Use:** When the user needs to blend features from up to three distinct data spaces (e.g., scalar, gradient, curvature) and hardware constraints prevent higher-dimensional textures.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the classification requires more than three unique dimensions; when higher precision in multi-dimensional blending is required.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of transfer functions and the size of the 3D texture [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the size of the 3D texture ($R^3 \times 4$ bytes) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Limited by hardware texture constraints. Performance is generally good for up to 3D textures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Intuitive specification for users with a priori knowledge of the data [S1, unofficial]. Allows blending of transfer functions from different spaces [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot handle more than three unique dimensions [S1, unofficial]. Less powerful than true multi-dimensional transfer functions if heuristics are used [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When the visualization requires combining transfer functions from up to three different data spaces and hardware 3D textures are available [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When more than three unique dimensions are required, or when a heuristic approach is insufficient for the desired level of detail [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from the work on interactive transfer function specification for direct volume rendering, specifically addressing the challenge of specifying complex mappings when there is a priori knowledge of the data. It was developed to allow users to intuitively explore features enhanced by different transfer functions and to generate complex mappings by aggregating several simpler ones. The approach was found adequate for the volumes used in the experiments, balancing expressiveness with hardware limitations [S1, unofficial].
+This method was introduced in the context of interactive transfer function specification for direct volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the need for users to intuitively explore features enhanced by different transfer functions and to generate complex mappings by aggregating simpler ones [S1, unofficial]. The approach is based on the hardware limitations of texture units at the time of its development [S1, unofficial].
 
